/**
\mainpage %Pixel Dust Game Engine (PDG)

PDG is an open-source 2D game engine with C++ and JavaScript APIs. It provides
rendering, sprites, animation, input, audio, resources, events, and optional
physics. You write the application and game rules; PDG supplies the runtime
systems that run them.

This is the **C++ API reference** for the current source tree. Start with
<a href="topics.html">Topics</a> to browse by task, use the
<a href="annotated.html">class index</a> to find a type, or switch to the
<a href="../../javascript/html/index.html">JavaScript introduction</a>.

\section cxx_start Start here

| What you want to do | Where to start |
| --- | --- |
| Create a window and draw | pdg::GraphicsManager, pdg::Port, \ref Graphics |
| Style shapes, images, and text | pdg::Attributes and pdg::AnimatedAttributes; use pdg::Drawing for reusable artwork |
| Organize a scene | pdg::SpriteLayer, pdg::Sprite, pdg::TileLayer, \ref Sprites |
| Move objects or animate a character | pdg::Animated and \ref Animation |
| Respond to input and engine notifications | pdg::IEventHandler, pdg::EventEmitter, pdg::EventManager, \ref Events |
| Load resources, schedule work, or play audio | \ref Managers and \ref Sound |
| Save or transmit structured state | pdg::ISerializable, pdg::Serializer, pdg::Deserializer, \ref Serialization |
| Add simulation or native networking | \ref Physics and \ref Network; optional \ref native_network |

\section cxx_model How an application fits together

A pdg::Port is a drawing surface. For custom graphics, respond to
pdg::eventType_PortDraw and issue drawing commands through the supplied port.
pdg::Attributes carries colors, gradients, strokes, text and image settings, and
transforms. pdg::Drawing stores reusable elements; pdg::ElementRef provides
explicit editing access. Drawing elements currently copy their Attributes.

Sprites provide managed scene objects. A Sprite belongs to a SpriteLayer, whose
transform and ordering apply to its sprites. The engine handles their normal
animation and drawing. Schedule motion on managed objects rather than also
stepping them yourself each frame.

For example, with an existing Sprite in an active layer:

\code{.cpp}
sprite.moveTo(pdg::Point(240, 180), 0.5, pdg::easeInOutQuad);
sprite.rotateBy(1.0f, 0.5, pdg::easeInOutQuad,
              pdg::rotationDirection_Clockwise);
\endcode

These two operations move and rotate the sprite over half a second. Custom
application drawing remains separate from its programmed motion.

The optional MVC application framework adds controllers, views, dialogs, and
controls. See `src/inc/pdg/app/` and the native example
`test/cxx/app-control-gallery.cpp` for layout, input handling, and custom controls.

\section cxx_animation Character animation

PDG supports image-frame playback and Spriter SCML animation. The Sprite pose
APIs add clip sampling and seeking, blends and transitions, bone/binding
transforms, procedural modifiers, two-bone IK, spring targets, contact locks,
and custom Drawing artwork attached to a pose. Authored triggers are delivered
through pdg::eventType_SpriteTriggerEvent. Optional Chipmunk integration supports
physical animation rigs and recovery to authored animation.

The \ref Animation topic explains the workflow and coordinate spaces. Enable
pose evaluation with pdg::Sprite::enableAnimationPose() before using the
pose-based extensions, and check its result: the pose adapter supports a
narrower rig contract than ordinary Spriter playback.

pdg::Part currently supplies an independently animated transform, identity,
hierarchy, and optional bone binding under a Sprite. Create it with
pdg::Sprite::createPart(); an unbound part returns pdg::boneId_None from
pdg::Part::getBoneId(). Sprite and Part expose an optional pdg::PhysicsBody through
the read-only physics member; setupPhysicsBody() instantiates it explicitly.
Compare sprite.physics with pdg::PhysicsBody::NoPhysics to test for absence.
All absent associations point to that one shared object. Programmed movement/spin and sizing remain on
Animated; mass, velocity, damping, forces and impulses belong to PhysicsBody.
Sprite bodies use Chipmunk on enabled layers or PDG's basic integrator otherwise.
Part bodies currently use the basic integrator; authored artwork and Part
contacts/constraints remain in development.

\section cxx_conventions Timing and API conventions

- Animation durations and elapsed steps use **floating-point seconds**: `0.25`
  means a quarter second. This includes Animated motion, sprite fades, layer
  fades/zoom, animation helpers, and easing callbacks.
- Angles are radians. Timed rotations accept integer rotation-direction
  constants; pose and Part coordinate spaces also use integer enum constants.
- Frame numbers are integers, frame-playback rates are frames per second, and
  blend weights are dimensionless.
- General timers and event timestamps retain their documented millisecond
  units. Convert an elapsed millisecond interval with `/ 1000.0` before passing
  it to an animation step. \ref animation_timing lists remaining boundaries.
- Ownership differs between value types, managed objects, and retained
  references. Follow each class's factory and lifetime contract, especially
  when keeping pose views or callback data after a callback returns.

\section cxx_examples Build and explore

The native build uses CMake and requires a C++20 compiler and standard library,
including `std::format`. The iOS application requires **iOS 16.3 or later**. POSIX checkouts use `./configure` and
`make`; Windows checkouts use `configure.ps1` and `make.ps1`. Initialize Git
submodules when obtaining the source. The native control gallery is available
as the CMake target `pdg-app-control-gallery` in a GUI build. Its source shows
application initialization, event handling, controls, and custom drawing.

With the desktop JavaScript runtime built, run these examples from the repository
root:

\code{.sh}
./test/demo spriter
./test/demo mvc
\endcode

The animation showcase includes ordinary and blended skeleton playback, mouse
aiming, a Drawing sword with spring recoil, and foot IK following a rising,
sliding rock. Let the opening zoom finish; press **I** to toggle foot IK.
The control gallery demonstrates the MVC application framework. Native
regressions live in `test/cxx/`; script specs live in `test/spec/`.
Run both with `./test/unit`, or select a CTest suite with
`./test/unit cpp:pdg-spriter-playback`. `./test/rigs` runs asset/controller
integration checks, `./test/ui` opens visual pages, `./test/tools` validates
test/build tooling, and `./test/perf` runs benchmarks. The source checkout
contains complete selection and platform instructions in `test/README.md`.

\section cxx_builds Runtime and build capabilities

The source tree includes desktop builds for macOS, Windows, and Linux, headless
builds, an iOS integration, and an Emscripten/WebAssembly browser build. These
targets do not expose identical services. Headless builds omit windowing and
graphics; audio and scripting facilities depend on the target. Consult the
target's build configuration when choosing an API.

CMake options include `PDG_HEADLESS`, `USE_SPRITER`, and `USE_CHIPMUNK`.
Spriter-specific Sprite APIs require Spriter support; physical rigs additionally
require Chipmunk. Query pdg::Sprite::supportsAnimationPhysics() and
pdg::Sprite::isAnimationDrawingSupported() where applicable. The reference
includes optional interfaces even when a particular build omits them.

\section api_stability API stability

Class notes describe compatibility commitments, independently of implementation
quality and platform support. The same public API has the same level in both
language references; language-specific wrappers may have their own rating.

| Level | Commitment |
| --- | --- |
| 1 — Experimental | Signatures, behavior or availability may change without a compatibility period. |
| 2 — Evolving | Implemented and supported; the contract is still settling. Compatibility is preserved where practical and breaking changes are documented in release notes. |
| 3 — Stable | Established public contract. Intentional breaking changes require an announced breaking release and migration guidance. |

Methods, options, event payloads and result structures inherit their owning API's
level unless separately marked. A stable Serializer/Deserializer interface does
not guarantee stable snapshot bytes: supported record versions, resource policies
and restoration restrictions are documented separately. Build requirements remain
in force at every level; animation physics requires Chipmunk.

The application framework's views, controls, application classes and appearance
classes are **Level 2 — Evolving** in both C++ and JavaScript. Its JavaScript guide
is `src/js/mvc-app/README.md`; C++ declarations live under `pdg/app/` and are not
included in this engine reference. Unrated utilities and internal implementation
types carry no new compatibility promise from this policy.

\section cxx_about About PDG

PDG began in 2003 with Ed Zavada at Pixel Dust Games for Catan Online, continued
at Dream Rock Studios, and was released under the MIT license in 2012. Its
focus remains reusable 2D engine and application systems. Game-specific AI,
pathfinding, rules, and content-authoring workflows belong to the application.
*/
